<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.head(
      title: "For developers — CSuiteFinder API",
      description: "A JSON API for work emails and direct phone numbers. $#{@email_price} an email, $#{@phone_price} a phone, billed only for answers. #{@trial_usd} of free credit to start.",
      nav: :developers,
      audience: :developer,
      css: CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.page_css(:developers),
      wrap_class: "wrap-wide"
    ) %>
<header>
  <h1>One endpoint, one price.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A plain JSON API over HTTPS for work emails and direct phone numbers. No SDK, no seat
    licence, no sales call. You pay for answers — a lookup that finds nothing is free.
  </p>
  <div class="hero-cta">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/start">Get started — <%= @trial_usd %> free credit</a>
    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>
  </div>
  <p class="cta-note">
    Buying seats for a sales team instead? <a href="/teams">That's the other plan →</a>
  </p>
</header>

<h2 class="section-label">Getting a key</h2>

<pre><code>curl -X POST <%= @base_url %>/csuitefinder/register \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '{"email": "you@company.com"}'</code></pre>

<p class="quiet">
  Send the key as <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;key&gt;</code> on every request. Prefer a
  browser? <a href="/account">Your account page</a> does the same thing, plus balance and billing.
</p>

<h3 class="sub-label">Example</h3>

<pre><code>curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $CSF_KEY" \
  "<%= @base_url %>/csuitefinder/email/find?full_name=Jensen%20Huang&domain=nvidia.com"

{
  "email": "jhuang@nvidia.com",
  "full_name": "Jensen Huang",
  "domain": "nvidia.com",
  "found": true,
  "confidence": 0.937638
}</code></pre>

<h2 class="section-label">Endpoints</h2>

<div class="table-scroll endpoints">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Path</th><th>Parameters</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>POST</td><td><code>/csuitefinder/company/people</code></td>
      <td><code>domain</code>, <code>department</code>, <code>limit</code></td>
      <td>Who works at a company, with phone numbers. <code>department=executive</code> for the C-suite. $<%= @phone_price %> per person.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>POST</td><td><code>/csuitefinder/email/company/people</code></td>
      <td><code>domain</code>, <code>department</code>, <code>limit</code></td>
      <td>The same sweep, email addresses only. $<%= @email_price %> per person.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>POST</td><td><code>/csuitefinder/phone/find</code></td>
      <td><code>full_name</code> + <code>domain</code>, or <code>email</code></td>
      <td>A direct phone number, with line type. $<%= @phone_price %>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>POST</td><td><code>/csuitefinder/phone/valid</code></td><td><code>phone</code></td>
      <td>Is the number real? Line type, carrier, region and every format.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>POST</td><td><code>/csuitefinder/phone/name</code></td><td><code>phone</code></td>
      <td>Whose number is this — from numbers we have already found for you.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>POST</td><td><code>/csuitefinder/phone/enrich</code></td><td><code>phone</code></td>
      <td>The person behind a number: name, title, employer, address.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>POST</td><td><code>/csuitefinder/phone/company</code></td><td><code>phone</code></td>
      <td>Which company a number belongs to.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>POST</td><td><code>/csuitefinder/email/find</code></td>
      <td><code>full_name</code>, <code>domain</code>, <code>email</code><span class="label"> (optional)</span></td>
      <td>A name and a company become that person's work email. The optional <code>email</code> is a colleague's address at the same company.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>POST</td><td><code>/csuitefinder/email/deliverable</code></td><td><code>email</code></td>
      <td>Is this mailbox real? Returns deliverable, undeliverable, risky or unknown, with the MX and SMTP signals behind it.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>POST</td><td><code>/csuitefinder/email/enrich</code></td><td><code>email</code></td>
      <td>The person behind an address — name, job title, seniority, department, employer, location.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>POST</td><td><code>/csuitefinder/email/name</code></td><td><code>email</code></td>
      <td>Just the identity: name, title, employer. The narrow form of enrich.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>POST</td><td><code>/csuitefinder/email/pattern</code></td><td><code>email</code></td>
      <td>How a company formats its addresses, with every alternative and how common each is.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>POST</td><td><code>/csuitefinder/company/find</code></td><td><code>email</code> or <code>domain</code></td>
      <td>Which company an address belongs to — name, domain, website, LinkedIn.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>POST</td><td><code>/csuitefinder/company/info</code></td><td><code>email</code> or <code>domain</code></td>
      <td>The full company profile: industry, headcount, founding year, location.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p class="quiet">
  Every endpoint also accepts <code>GET</code> with a query string. The optional
  <code>email</code> on <code>/email/find</code> is a colleague's address at the same company:
  give us one address there and we can resolve the rest.
</p>

<h3 class="sub-label">Managing your account</h3>

<div class="table-scroll">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>GET /csuitefinder/billing/balance</code></td><td>Your balance and the full price list</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>GET /csuitefinder/billing/usage?days=30</code></td><td>Calls, cache hits and spend per endpoint</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>GET /csuitefinder/keys</code></td><td>Your API keys — mint a spare, revoke an old one</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>POST /csuitefinder/billing/topup</code></td><td>Buy credit — returns a PayPal approval link</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>GET /csuitefinder/health</code></td><td>Service status</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2 class="section-label">Using it with an AI agent</h2>

<p class="quiet">
  The fastest way in — <a href="/start"><strong>the full walkthrough is here</strong></a>.
  If you work with Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor or any coding agent, you do not have to
  explain this API to it. Point it at our
  <a href="/llms.txt"><code>llms.txt</code></a> and it will know every endpoint,
  parameter and price — the file is generated from the live service, so what it
  reads is what you are charged.
</p>

<p class="quiet"><strong>Paste this to your agent:</strong></p>

<pre><code>Read <%= @base_url %>/llms.txt and use the CSuiteFinder API.
Register for a key, then find the work email for each person in my list.
Verify every address before you give it to me.</code></pre>

<p class="quiet">
  It will register itself, take the <%= @trial_usd %> of free credit and start working. If your
  agent supports remembering instructions, drop the same URL into its project rules
  or <code>AGENTS.md</code> and it will keep the API available for later.
</p>

<div class="note">
  <strong>Worth telling your agent:</strong> you can start from a name <em>or</em> from a
  domain. <code>/email/find</code> resolves someone you can already name;
  <code>/company/people</code> is the one that builds the list. Agents that only learn the
  first waste turns hunting for a search endpoint they have already been given.
</div>

<!-- ── pricing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     At the foot of the page on purpose: the nav's Pricing link is a bare
     #pricing fragment, so it scrolls down whichever page the visitor is on
     rather than crossing them over into the other audience's plan. -->
<h2 class="section-label" id="pricing">Pricing</h2>

<p class="quiet" style="margin-top:-6px">
  You pay for what we find: <strong>$<%= @email_price %> an email address</strong> and
  <strong>$<%= @phone_price %> a phone number</strong>, whether you resolve one person from a
  name or discover a roomful from a domain. Deliverability checks, identity lookups, company
  data and email formats are all included with any balance.
</p>

<div class="bundle-grid">
  <div class="bundle-card">
    <span class="amt">$<%= @email_price %></span>
    <span class="rate">per work email found</span>
  </div>
  <div class="bundle-card">
    <span class="amt">$<%= @phone_price %></span>
    <span class="rate">per phone number found</span>
  </div>
  <div class="bundle-card">
    <span class="amt">$0</span>
    <span class="rate">per lookup that finds nothing</span>
  </div>
</div>

<p class="quiet">
  Credit you buy <strong>never expires</strong> and a dollar buys a dollar of it. Purchases
  start at $<%= @min_bundle %> and go through PayPal. Start with
  <strong><%= @trial_usd %> of free credit</strong> — real lookups, not a preview. Trial
  credit lasts <%= @trial_months %> month.
</p>

<h3 class="sub-label">Per-endpoint cost</h3>

<div class="table-scroll">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Endpoint</th><th class="num">Price</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <%= for {endpoint, usd, label} <- @price_rows do %>
    <tr>
      <td><code><%= endpoint %></code></td>
      <td class="num"><%= if usd > 0, do: "$" <> label, else: "included" %></td>
    </tr>
    <% end %>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h3 class="sub-label">Seat plan, for comparison</h3>

<p class="quiet">
  If the people using this are working in a browser rather than writing code,
  a <a href="/teams">seat</a> is $<%= @seat_usd %> a month per person and grants
  $<%= @seat_usd %> of the same credit each month. Same prices per lookup; the
  seat's credit expires at the end of the month, bought credit does not.
</p>

<div class="table-scroll compare">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th></th><th class="seat">A seat — $<%= @seat_usd %>/month</th><th>Credit — bought up front</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <%= for row <- @comparison do %>
    <tr>
      <td><strong><%= row.question %></strong></td>
      <td class="seat"><%= row.seat %></td>
      <td><%= row.credit %></td>
    </tr>
    <% end %>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p style="margin-top:26px">
  <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/start">Get started — <%= @trial_usd %> free credit</a>
</p>

<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.footer(:developer) %>

</div>
</body>
</html>
